\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Paper   \u183 \'b7   ff. {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5706[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 i + 246}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26    \u183 \'b7   {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5360[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 208 x 145}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26    \u183 \'b7   Southern Germany   \u183 \'b7   1444}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Paper (thick; watermarks, in gutter: similar to Briquet Monts 11786 and unidentified bull\rquote s head), ff. i (f. 1) + 246, 208 x 145 mm. Size of written space varies as does the number of long lines of text. Frame-ruled in ink or hard point; remains of prickings in upper, outer, inner margins   \u183 \'b7   I\endash IX\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , X\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 16\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  (ff. 110\endash 125), XI\endash XII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , XIII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 16\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , XIV\endash XIX\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , XX\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . Quires II\endash IX signed on verso with Arabic numerals 1\endash 8, some trimmed; remainder of quires have catchwords, also trimmed   \u183 \'b7   Written by multiple scribes in varying styles of gothic hybrida and bookhand scripts   \u183 \'b7   Crude red initials, 3- to 2-line, throughout; ff. 33v\endash 38r, 113v\endash 114v and 166r\endash 203r rubricated.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written in Southern Germany in 1444 (see colophon, f. 239r). Perhaps produced and/or owned by the Benedictine abbey of Amorbach in Bavaria [K. S.: L. H. Cottineau, R\u233 \'e9pertoire topo-bibliographiques des abbayes et prieur\u233 \'e9s, 3 vol., M\u226 \'e2con 1939\endash 1970, see vol. 1, col. 88\endash 89]. According to A. Derolez (letter on file) the same roses that are painted on the edges of Marston MS 141 also occur (but on a larger scale) on the edges of the three volumes of the following printed book in the University Library, Ghent: Biblia latina glosata (Strasbourg: Adolf Rusch, ca. 1481; GKW 4282). These volumes are blind-stamped with the text-stamp \ldblquote Amorbach\rdblquote  and contain the ownership mark of this abbey; the roses are not, however, mentioned as characteristic of the Amorbach books in: P. Lehmann, Die Bibliothek des Klosters Amorbach, in: Erforschung des Mittelalters 3, Stuttgart 1960, pp. 76\endash 109. Purchased in 1946 from Heinrich Rosenthal by H. P. Kraus (\ldblquote 4055/UZR\rdblquote ), who sold it in 1957 to Thomas E. Marston (bookplate).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 
\par \pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 secundo folio: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Cum ergo}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Faye and Bond, p. 80, no. 141   \u183 \'b7   Barbara A. Shailor, The Medieval Book. Illustrated from the Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library (catalogue of an exhibition held Aug. 15 \endash  Oct. 31, 1988), Toronto 1991, p. 60 (reproduction of binding).}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Binding: Germany, s. xv. The backs of the quires are cut in. Original sewing on three double supports is laced into almost flush wooden boards, and the tawed skin cores of braided endbands, sewn through the cover, are also laced. The spine is back cornered with lining extending between supports on the outside of the boards. Large vermilion and sepia roses are painted on each edge (see also provenance)   \u183 \'b7   Back pastedown (and perhaps the inner front pastedown, covered by paper) consists of a parchment bifolium (Germany, s. XIII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ) containing the \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs22 Sermones de tempore\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  of \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs22 Johannes Halgrinus de Abbatisvilla\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  (Schneyer, v. 3, p. 512, nos. 26\endash 27). Ca. 31 long lines, written space ca. 150 x 90 mm. Written in small neat early gothic bookhand, above top line. Binding stays from this and other parchment manuscripts, s. xiii\endash xiv   \u183 \'b7   Covered in kermes pink skin blind-tooled with an \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 X\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  in a frame on the front board, tying-up marks on the spine, and a frame on the lower one. Five round, brass bosses on each board and one fastening, the catch inset on the upper board, the lower one cut in for the strap.}

