\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 1r\endash 24v \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Reformatio Sigismundi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . Text: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Allmechtiger schopffer himels vnd des ertrichs, gib kraft vnd tuo genad\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 \endash  \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 sy tragen in dem kor schetter, daz in die mentel singent vnd lesent\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 \u9553 \'a6 \endash  Originally written in 1439 at the alleged instigation of the Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund (1368\endash 1437) for the Council of Basel. The text of Marston MS 273 is the vulgate version (V), composed around 1440; for the text of all versions, see H. Koller, ed., Reformation Kaiser Siegmunds (MGH Staatsschriften des sp\u228 \'e4teren Mittelalters VI), Stuttgart 1964. The text of Marston MS 273 breaks off at p. 213 of this edition. As with other versions, the author of this text is supposedly Friedrich von Lantnau or Lantzenau (see f. 6r), who claims to have undertaken the translation of it into German from the \ldblquote original\rdblquote  Latin. The identity of this person is still uncertain (see Koller, op. cit., p. 6), and the idea of a Latin \ldblquote original\rdblquote  is rejected by the editor (Koller, op. cit., p. 17).}

