\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 1r) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.1: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Rasis or Aristotle:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Lumen luminum perfecti magisterii\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . Headline, 1 (in a fifteenth-century gothic cursive hand): \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Secreta Secretorum Aristotelis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; (f. 1r) headline, 2 (in a fifteenth-century prehumanistic hand, black ink): \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Versus: Felix qui potuit rerum cognoscere causas:\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (f. 1r1, 1) >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Secretum secretorum Aristotilis.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Cum studij sollertis indagine\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 3r1, 31) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 concludes congruo. et exinde mercedem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 laboris invenies decuplatam. Explicit\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 \endash  First section only, substantially as printed by Gratarolus, 1572, II, pp. 101\endash 104; Ruska, Osiris VII, pp. 45\endash 46; TK col. 344; DWS no. 114; Corbett I, 28, etc. The general introduction to the text found here and frequently ascribed to Rasis was apparently first printed in: Vera alchymiae doctrina, 1561, II, 188 and following. See also 2.17, ff. 69r1\endash 76r2, for the remainder of this text.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

