\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 4r1, 27) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.3: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Anonymous:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Liber sacerdotum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , extracts. >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Liber contracionum.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ex antiquorum scientia\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 philosophorum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends f. 5v2, 24: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 accipe de limatura\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 martis et admisce confectioni cum fusa fuerit.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  TK col. 1618; or, Liber communicationum, a title erroneously read from this manuscript, TK col. 530; also possibly related to Liber coniunctionum, attributed to Alquinus, TK col. 530. With the exception of an unidentified paragraph, Magnesiam feminam, on f. 5v1, the material in this section is closely similar to that printed by Berthelot, Chimie I, nos. 1\endash 18, 20, 19, 21\endash 25, 149, 153. The text begins with a wording parallel to that in the work attributed to Alquinus (also attributed to others; vide TK col. 1618; DWS III, pp. 1124\endash 1125), but the closing supplied by DWS is not in the present manuscript. The connection of the unidentified paragraphs which follow is therefore uncertain.}

