\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 37r) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.11: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Geber:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Summa perfectionis magisterii\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . Headline (written in a bold fifteenth-century Bastarda): \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Summa Geber perfectionis magisterij liber primus.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (f. 37r1, 1) >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Incipit Geber.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Totam nostram\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\cf17  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 scientiam quam ex libris antiquorum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 64v1, 1) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 inquisicionem hec dicta sufficiant. Explicit summa Gebri\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [altered by insertion to \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Geberi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 perfectionis magisterij. Deo gratias. Amen. Incipit summa Gebri perfectionis magisterij. Totam nostram scientiam\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 64v2, 49) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De preparacione martis.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 \u9553 \'a6 Ends abruptly. \endash  TK col. 1576; DWS no. 105, etc. The text is quite complete and varies only slightly from the printed editions, Verae alchemiae, 1561, I, pp. 118\endash 184, etc. In this copy, the chapter headings are repeated after the explicit, breaking off slightly imperfectly at the end of f. 64v2, at which point at least one quire of the manuscript has been excised.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red255\green102\blue0;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 65r1, 1) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.12: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Anonymous: Liber solis et lunae\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . (f. 65r1, 1\endash 4) Blank, writing has been washed out on these lines and is now illegible. A note, ca. 1700, line 5: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ars solis et Lunae.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (f. 65r1, 6) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 In nomine domini Amen. Incipit ars solis et lune\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 idest auri et argenti verissima et sine dubio in\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 omnis examinatione stabilis usque ad finem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 et perfecta\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 65v2, 42) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Et habes aurum multo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 melius omni arabye et naturali.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  A short treatise, noted by TK col. 727 from the Art Ancien description of this manuscript only. The procedure includes a drawing of apparatus on f. 65v1.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red255\green102\blue0;}

