\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 65v2, 44) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.13: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Anonymous:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Liber de oleis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Incipit liber de oleis et primo de oleo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 capillorum.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Accipe\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .4. vel .5. libras de fortissimo capitello facto de cineribus clavellatis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 67r1, 14) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 hoc quod remanebit\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 in fundo vasis.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  Noted by TK col. 23 from the Art Ancien catalogue description of this codex, and with a seemingly false reference to Ernst Darmstaedter\rquote s Die Alkemie des Gebers, Berlin 1922. The text concerning oils appears to end at the point cited, followed by a series of recipes not connected with it as follows.}

