\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 69r1, 1) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.17: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Rasis or Aristotle:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Lumen luminum perfecti magisterii\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Incipit secunda racio\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 .< The rubric is written to the right. (f. 69r1, 1) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Omnia corpora\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 que a lunari globo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 The text begins to the left; ends f. 76r2, 8: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 aque dulcis preparate et utere de eo sicut praedixi. Finitur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 liber perfecti magisterij Aristotelis.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  All but the opening section, TK col. 344; DWS no. 114. The opening section of the text stands first in the present codex, f. 1, where further references are given. Versions of the text vary greatly; the ending in this codex is much like that in London, B.M. Sloane 1754, cited by DWS and Ruska, Osiris VII, pp. 55\endash 56. See also TK col. 989.}

