\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 87r2, 6) \plain \f1\caps\lang1033\fs26 2.25: \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Anonymous:\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26  Alchemy in thirty chapters\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Incipit liber quartus qui per quendam\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 socium ex diversis doctoribus est collectus et compositus. Primo de sublimacionibus.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Mercurius sic sublimatur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 In the right margin in red: >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Capitulum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 primum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 .< (f. 88v2, 47) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Et postea conmisceatur cum fuligine ferri et\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 sublimetur sal ab eo.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  TK col. 732, noted only from the Art Ancien catalogue listing. It is not certain whether the text originally ended at this point, nor whether this final text is complete as it stands in the codex. \endash  f. 88v lower margin: At the extreme right, in a later hand, perhaps that which wrote the \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ex biblioth. Salmasij\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  inscription on the lower pastedown, vide infra, is the following: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 scripsit Frater Bartholomaeus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ol\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [\u8230 \'85] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 anno domini\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 mccczzzv\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic]. To the left, i.e., in the center of the lower margin, three lines of writing, possibly in the same hand, are faintly visible, though erased and washed out; they do not fluoresce well and have not been read.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red255\green102\blue0;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Lower pastedown (in a bold Bastarda hand, now completely faded out but visible under ultraviolet light, is the inscription:) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Presens liber constat xij florenos.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  Partly covering the above is a reading note of Henricus Cornelius. At the foot, beneath nine staves of plainsong notation, described in the section above on the binding, is a recipe in a fifteenth-century Bastarda, faint, highly abbreviated, and not transcribed; below it is written in a late hand, possibly seventeenth century: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ex biblioth.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [eca] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Salmasij\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 .}

