\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Probably original binding of brown calf, the covers ruled with triple parallel lines to a pattern of four rectangles within a rectangle, the larger rectangle crossed with similar ruling; indications of five center and corner pieces on each cover, possibly of iron and certainly fastened with iron nails, now lost; indications of two missing clasps and catches at the fore-edges of the covers; heavily repaired at fore-edges, hinges, and backstrip, the original back divided into four compartments by five heavy double bands, a modern morocco label in the second compartment from the topgold-stamped between double gold rules top and bottom: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 ALCHEMICAL\endash MEDICAL\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 MISCELLANY\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  \endash  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 MANUSCRIPT\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 MIDDLE EUROPE\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 XVTH CENTURY\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 .}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Front pastedown: Worn and damaged, and with rust stains from iron nails which have penetrated the cover from the various attachments and cornerpieces; contains notes in Latin and German in several fifteenth- and early sixteenth-century hands, not transcribed, consisting of recipes, notes, and a brief quotation from Aristotle\rquote s Probleumata, the next to last entry apparently dated 1521 at the end. At the extreme left center is a faint note in a much later hand: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Collegij Soc\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ietatis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Iehs\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 u\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Rosonij\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (?) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 1666\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . The place intended might be Rosnya = Rosenau in Hungary, but reading and interpretation are uncertain.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 3r, 1) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.1: Bartholomaeus St\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 \u228 \'e4\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 ber, Recipe against Syphilis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin. (ff. 1\endash 2) Lacking. The leaves of the first quire were left unfoliated by the copyist-compiler. (f. 3r) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Contra Emorroydas 13a\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De elementis 157..b. Emula campana 143..z.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends f. 9r, 31: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De zinziber vidi 139. .7.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [i.e., a conventional sign from the printer\rquote s alphabet] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 et 142 .k.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  This consists of an alphabetical index by the copyist to the contents of the codex, ending f. 198r (186\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), in which citations are made to the double-opening folio numbers provided by the scribe with a system of marginal letter-symbols, used to mark subjects cited. The index begins with a portion of the entries under the letter \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 E\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , the earlier part of the alphabet having occupied all or parts of the now missing first two leaves. Many of the blank spaces in the index leaves are filled up with medicinal recipes in Latin and German, not transcribed, by the same hands, or similar ones, which made the pastedown entries already mentioned. One of these notes near the foot of f. 7r begins: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vnguentum doctoris staber ad ulcera atque\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 uulnera si superpositum fuerit mirifice conducens.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  Then follows a list of ingredients; the source of this recipe is very likely Bartholomaeus St\u228 \'e4ber (this spelling is used by Klebs and TK, while the original text provides \ldblquote St\u235 \'ebb\u235 \'ebr\rdblquote , and BMC uses \ldblquote Steber\rdblquote ), whose tract on preservation from and cure of syphilis (A Malafranczos morbogallorum preservatio ac Cura a Bartholomeo Steber Viennensi artium et medicine doctore) was printed at Vienna about 1497\endash 98 (TK 1364; Klebs 931.1; BMC Inc. III, p. 811); vide infra, f. 218v, where substantially the same matter is repeated with clear identification of the source.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 9v) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.2: Nicolaus (?), Versus de ponderibus medicinarum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . (f. 9v, 1) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vncia\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [with superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .z.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Libra\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .1.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Scrupulus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .3.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Dragma\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .z.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \u8230 \'85 (line 5, after one-line space:) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Scrupulus est pondus 20 granorum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Brief unidentified notes on the conventional signs for weights and their relative values. Ends line 8. (f. 9v, 6) [crossed \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 w\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , meaning Versus ?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Collige triteis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 medicine pondera granis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (line 15) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Librarum quinque debes pond us mediare\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \endash  TK 234, here in nine verses. As pointed out by TK, the short text was printed in 1496 at Venice, at the end of a longer work by Dinus de Garbo, GW 8347 (not GW 8342), reprinted in 1499, GW 8348, and perhaps subsequently. Occurring in manuscripts of the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, these verses may be related to the writings of Nicolaus on weights and measures in medicine. Compare MS 15, described below.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 9v, 16) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.3: Anonymous, Verses on a useful poultice against gout\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin, unidentified. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Tabula Tunc embrotamus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 cum membra liquore rigamus De pannis in adidis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 est fomentacio sola\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends line 23. Below, a recipe in Latin and German, \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Contra podagram\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , not transcribed, one of a large number against this disease added by one or more of the hands which have made additional notes in the volume.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 10r, 1) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.4: Anonymous\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 , Descriptions of urines\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin. Added by one of the cursive hands about 1500 to pages originally left blank by the scribe: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vrina habens superius densum circulum significat\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [? with superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 t\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 prout debile et gravedinem in fronte Vrina clara et habens circulum superius rubeum significat nimius sanguinem in anteriore parte capitis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Twenty-eight descriptions of urines of different characters are written through the final line of f. 10v, where a description breaks off defectively, with a short catch phrase below suggesting that the tract was continued on a portion or the whole of the two following leaves, now missing. Incomplete at the end, not identified.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 14r[2\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.5: Anonymous, Flores Alchimiae\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin. Headline: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Flores alchimie \u182 \'b6 Nota exponens Gebritales\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Accipe argentumvivum per sublimacionem deputatum vel purgatum quattuor\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 et vltima vice cum alumine calcinato \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 \u8230 \'85 Ends f. 26r. \u8230 \'85 (f. 26r[14\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 14 \u182 \'b6 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ad verucas. Recipe agrimonia, et gallas quercinas cum sale et aceto terratur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Though the headline \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Flores alchimie\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  was omitted on f. 16r(4\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) and abandoned after f. 17r(5\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), the whole section consists of shorter and longer recipes and procedures; for a few, the passage on f. 14v(3\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) attributed to Calisthenes, that on f. 23r(11\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) attributed to Magister Julianus, and another on f. 26r(14\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) naming Gilbertus, sources have been given, but they have remained unidentified. The short poem beginning \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Artus est hominis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85, which occurs on f. 15r(3\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), is also found in MSS 5.16 and 11.2 of the catalogue, q.v., but has not been located in the literature consulted. The collection closes with a group of procedures for producing startling effects, comparable but less numerous and probably not directly related to those already described in MS 6. \endash  ff. 11\endash 12 lacking. It has already been explained in the technical description of MS 9 that all of the leaves from the second through the twenty-third quires inclusive were foliated in an unusual fashion by the original scribe: f. 1r of the second quire, i.e., f. 13r which would follow immediately if the leaf were not lacking, was foliated \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , but its verso and the following recto, i.e., ff. 13v and 14 r, were both foliated \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , while each page of the next double-opening consisting of ff. 14v and 15r was foliated \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 3\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , and so forth through the last such pair in quire twenty-nine, formed by ff. 275v and 276r, both foliated \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 264\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . Instead of leaving the last page of this section f. 276v unnumbered, the copyist foliated it \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 265\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , but he did not foliate its facing page which marks the beginning of a new quire and a new section of the codex, nor did he foliate any of the remaining leaves in the manuscript. In the description following the correct folio numbers according to the collation of the codex are supplied first and are outside parentheses, while the scribe\rquote s foliation, where it occurs, of each verso side is indicated within parentheses by the scribe\rquote s double-opening number with a superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , each such recto side being cited similarly in parentheses with number and superscript \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , as in the citation immediately below and thenceforth until termination of the original foliation. ff. 13r(1)\endash 13v(2\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) lacking.}

