\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 52v[41\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 29) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.14: Anonymous, Vocabulary of herbs\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin and Czech. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vocabula herbarum. Accotus cossatecz, Altea Wyssoky flez, Anetum kopr Acedula. Styewyk\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 53v[42\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 8) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vngla kerhart, Marubium chebdye, Lupinus wlczymak, Olibanum byele hadydlo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . At this point the vocabulary breaks off abruptly, and some recipes in Latin follow, beginning at once in the same line 9: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Satrion quem allij, papisium dicunt quia in radice habet duos testiculos\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 The passage ends line 22. This is followed by a passage on remedies against fevers and chills and another on weights, not transcribed, all ending on f. 54r(43\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 11. \endash  Possibly related to the herbal of \plain \f2\lang1024\fs26 K\u345 \'f8i\u353 \'9a\u357 \'9d\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 an z Prachatic which follows only a page later in the codex, but not identified, closing with short sections on remedies against fevers and chills and on weights.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 53v[42\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 23) \plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 9.15: Christian of Prachaticz (K\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u345 \'f8\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 i\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u353 \'9a\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u357 \'9d\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 an z Prachatic), Herbularium\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Contra omnes febres et frigora.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  The rubric is also repeated in black; ends line 36. (f. 54r[42\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 1) Without rubric: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Notandum de ponderibus medicinarum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends line 11. \endash  Unidentified. (f. 54r[42\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 12) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Secuntur Virtutes herbarum. SJ quis herbarum vires breviter velit cognoscere\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 73v[62\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 7) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 auget sperma et confert memorie\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 \endash  TK 1250, cited from Paris BN MS latin 11231, ff. 12r\endash 70r; other anonymous MSS also noted by TK 1460. A. P. Gregor (in:\plain \f2\lang1024\fs26  Rukov\u283 \'ect \u269 \'e8esk\u233 \'e9ho jazyka a \u269 \'e8eskoslovensk\u233 \'e9\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ho p\u237 \'edsemnictv\u237 \'ed, Prague 1937, p. 253) states that \plain \f2\lang1024\fs26 K\u345 \'f8i\u353 \'9a\u357 \'9dan\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (d. 1439) was \ldblquote mistr\rdblquote  (i.e., a master or professor) of Prague University and cites his Latin herbal, with Czech names of plants, first among the author\rquote s writings. In MS 9, the text occurs in Latin, but the scribe of the MS has supplied Czech equivalents in the lateral margins, together with some Polish equivalents as well, invariably designated \ldblquote polsky\rdblquote .}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 73v[62\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 7) \plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 9.16: Christian of Prachaticz (K\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u345 \'f8\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 i\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u353 \'9a\u357 \'9d\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 an z Prachatic)\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , or more likely \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Nicolaus, Antidotarium\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Secuntur De Antidotario Nicolai. Aurea allexandrina, Aurea dicta est ab auro, Alexandrina\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 81r[69\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 16) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Et si non sufficit, tunc unam aliam vice et sufficit, et non pluries.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  TK 165, also noted from BN 11231 cited above, ff. 70v\endash 106v; TK has noted a text with this incipit following Christian\rquote s Herbularium in a single manuscript, as it does here; but here the attribution is the usual one to Nicolaus, which appears to be sufficient cause for querying the attribution in the BN MS. Gregor, loc. cit., mentions recipe books translated from Razes and Saliceto, but nothing which can be identified with the present text.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 81r[69\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 17) \plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 9.17: Christian of Prachaticz (K\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u345 \'f8\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 i\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u353 \'9a\u357 \'9d\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 an z Prachatic\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Lekarstwye\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , i.e., \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Medicines\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , or \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Prescriptions\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Czech. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Tato gssu lekarstwye mistra Krzystanowa:\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (one-line space) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 TEzmy naprwe pryskyrzyze. wosku. a. stare ho\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends abruptly, f. 88r(76\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 7: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 pyz rano aweczer\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  This little-known treatise in Czech may coincide with \plain \f2\lang1024\fs26 K\u345 \'f8i\u353 \'9a\u357 \'9dan\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 \rquote s L\u233 \'e9\plain \f2\lang1024\fs26 ka\u345 \'f8sk\u233 \'e9\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 kniehy, noted by Gregor, loc. cit., who does not supply the incipit.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 88r[76\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 11) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.18: Alexius Africus, De septem herbis et septem planetis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De septem herbis sequitur. Allexius affricus, discipulus bellonis, claudeo arthemensi epilogiturus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \u8230 \'85 (f. 91r[79\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 28) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Preceptum Ypocratis est quod serves illud nec doceas quemquam nec vlli reveles.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  Remainder of the page blank. \endash  TK 79; DWS III, pp. 769\endash 771; T II, pp. 233\endash 234; etc. As shown in DWS, the endings of different versions vary greatly; the ending in this manuscript resembles her specimen text, in which she has combined the best features of several versions. Cf. her note on the publication of a version of the text, Paris, 1888.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 91v[80\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]) \plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 9.19: Christian of Prachaticz (K\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u345 \'f8\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 i\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 \u353 \'9a\u357 \'9d\plain \f2\scaps\lang1024\fs26 an z Prachatic), A tract on days of the year to be treated with caution\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin, apparently unrecorded. Headline: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Magistri Cristanni\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (line 1) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Januarius habet .v. dies, primam in Crucifixione. Secundam die seguenti Terciam in sexta epyphanie. Quartam die seguenti, Quintam in die Mauri Abbatis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Istis diebus caveat homo. ne locum mutet, vel ducat uxorem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 92r[80\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 4) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 nec opus aliquid incipiat, quia ad nullum bonum perveniet.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f2\fnil\fcharset238 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 92r[80\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 6) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.20: Anonymous, Medical recipes\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin, not identified. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De Balneo. Corporibus repletur et pingwibus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \u8230 \'85 This marks the beginning of a series of miscellaneous recipes and general medical advice ending f. 97v(86\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 16: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Cui est honor et gloria in secula seculorum Amen.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 97v[86\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 17) \u182 \'b6 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Aqua omnium aquarum. Pone litargium\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 album\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 98r[86\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 9) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ad faciendum Cinobrium.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 98r[86\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 21) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Item alia separacio Auri ab argento. \u182 \'b6 Nym oleum sulfuris und las, das czu gen mit dem golde\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 \endash  This procedure is throughout in German, but is followed at once by further alchemical procedures in Latin, breaking off abruptly with a passage on \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 lunaria\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  which has been canceled by crisscross scoring with the pen on f. 103v(92\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), and continuing onto the uncanceled first line of the following page, the remainder of which was originally left blank; it and the following f. 104v(93\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) have subsequently been filled with notes in Latin and German by several sixteenth-century hands. \endash  ff. 105r(93\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 )\endash 108v(97\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) ruled and foliated but otherwise blank.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 109r[97\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 1) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.21: Anonymous, Alchemical procedures\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin, German, and Czech, not identified. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Dixit Magister Ia\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [cobus ?]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 . Quod quadam muliere generit laborante in eo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (line 5) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 In hoc casu valet nasturcium, Eyn Cress. \u182 \'b6 Item mulier Si masculum concipit rubea est Si vero feminam concipit pallida est.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  The text abruptly abandons the theme of conception and continues in general medicine, sometimes as at f. 109v(98\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 23, breaking into Czech; at f. 110r(98\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 8, the following occurs: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Magister Giserus de willa Parisiensis dicit, Grana. Imperij vij vel novem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 The shorter and longer recipes continue, mostly in Latin with occasional passages having Czech equivalents; a recipe on f. 116r(104\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) is attributed to Magister Marcus; ends f. 119v(108\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 27.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 119v[108\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 28) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.22: Albicus, De regimine sanitatis (de paralisi et pestilentia)\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Anno domini Millesimo .cccc. ijo currente hoc collegit Reverendus dominus Albicus in Wrat\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [islava ?]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Sciatis quod medicine in omnibus morbis diversificantur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 On f. 123v(112\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 1, and following occurs a \ldblquote Regimen Regis Wenceslay \u8230 \'85 pie memorie,\rdblquote  compiled (or this copy written ?) after the death of the monarch in 1419. (f. 127r[115\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 13) \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Hoc. Reverendus Albicus Archiepiscopus. Olym pragensis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 There are several further mentions of Albicus, sometimes with passages in Czech, once with the statement on f. 134r(126\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 19: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ista magister Albicus fecit Regi Bohemie\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Seemingly a new division of the text, \ldblquote De pestilencia,\rdblquote  begins at f. 141r(129\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 1; \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Magister Allexander yspanicus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (see also f. 160r[148\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 1) is mentioned at f. 145r(133\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 13, but Albicus\rquote  name recurs on f. 150v(139\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ); the text apparently ends at f. 153v(142\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ), 26: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Si fuerit in hyeme, tunc ponatis ad Stubam\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  TK 1391, partly composed in 1402 and before 1419 according to the text, possibly with additions not found in other copies, some of which are in Czech.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 153v[142\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 27) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 9.23: Anonymous, Virtutes herbarum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin. \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Virtutes herbarum sequntur. Galganum est siccum et calidum et habet has virtutes, primavirtus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 159v[148\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ], 30) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Item dolorem pectoris mitigat potata. Et sic est finis hoc totius.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  With occasional Czech equivalents in this copy, TK 575, noted from a fourteenth-century Prague manuscript and two others preserved at Munich.}

