\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 f. 10r, 1 \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Anonymous, Alchemical compilation\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin, with later additions in Latin and German, apparently the holograph of the original compiler, not identified. >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Opus nobile de anima seu aqua\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [of Lead]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Recipe laminas\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [of Lead] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 mundas et pone eas in cucurbita\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 (f. 11r, 9) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Et nota quod aqua quam super vocavi animam\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [of Lead] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 vocatur etiam aqua\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [of Lead]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Et potest fieri de\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [Tin] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 eodem modo ut de\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [Lead]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  Further related procedures follow. Ends f. 11v, 15: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 et tunc ponatur super furnellum ad fixandum.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Vide de hoc latius inter fixationes cinobrij.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  The fixations of Mercury are found on ff. 53\endash 55. On f. 11r, 15 is the mention: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 hoc\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 dicit sanctus Thomas in florido suo.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  The text continues with processes involving tin through f. 13r, 13, while the remainder of ff. 13\endash 14 are blank; beginning of f. 15r are recipes for the softening of iron and other metals; f. 16v is blank, followed by hardening procedures. On f. 17v a procedure by \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quidam alius in traiecto\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  and another by \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quidam monachus de Anglia\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  are mentioned. Various treatments of metals including rubification and albification follow. On the inserted slip f. 35-bis is a note: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 H. de traiecto mose\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 dixit mihi quod arsenicum crudum misit pulverizatum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 An \ldblquote albificatio\rdblquote  written on f. 36r cites Joannes du Bois as its author; on the inserted slip f. 36-bis is a recipe including the mention: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Magister Joannes de Herr sic dissolvit\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [Tin] \u8230 \'85; on f. 37r is a passage canceled by dotting beneath the lines which attributes another albification to Joannes du Bois, and still another similar recipe is attributed to Magister Johannes Borchle on f. 38r; ff. 41v\endash 46v are blank. On f. 47r begins a series of twelve \ldblquote Congelationes Mercurii,\rdblquote  one of them on f. 47v with the mention that: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Magister Nicolas dixit mihi pro\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 magno secreto quod\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [Mercury] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 amalgamatus cum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [Silver] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 congelatur et fixatur cum predicta medicina.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  On f. 48-bis is a recipe ending with what appears to be the name of the source: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ruychroc\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; this, however, has been canceled with the note at the end in darker ink: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Mendacium est, Bertrandus probavit.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  On the inserted slip f. 49-bis two procedures are attributed to Petrus de Delft and Dominus de Barbenson. A great many recipes, nearly all of them metallurgical, follow. In a section on Oils of Silver on f. 79v is a repetition of the recipe supplied to the compiler by Magister Nicolas written earlier on f. 47v. On f. 75v is a marginal recipe for another Oil of Silver with a note of the source: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Ex antiquario Iudoci\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; the same source may have been indicated on f. 35-bis in an incomplete note which seems to read: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ex antiquo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [?] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 libro Iudoci\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , so that it is uncertain whether Iudocus was an antiquarian or the possessor of an old book. A long series of fixations begins on f. 80r, ending on f. 98r, 10: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 et erit probatum in omni\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 examine. Antonius van der Kandt.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  It is uncertain whether Antonius was the author of the whole section or, what is perhaps more likely, only the last procedure in the group. Beginning on f. 99r is a collection of sixteen procedures involving transmutation into silver and gold and for making the philosophers\rquote  stone. Frequent changes of ink and stylus, soiling of the paper, and slight damage to edges and corners, as noted in the Summary, suggest that this quire was kept unbound over a more or less long period of time, and that different recipes were entered into it as attempted in the laboratory. Most are long and very complex, and all are anonymous, although Nicolaus of Cues is mentioned on f. 129v. Ends f. 133r, 11: \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 tunc eius partem unam\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 super mille partes metallorum imperfectorum.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  The remainder of the writing in the codex is by eighteenth-century German hands, probably one individual, writing a German cursive for the passages in German and an italic for Latin passages. On ff. 133v\endash 136v this hand has supplied an index of contents to the volume; f. 137 is blank, as are ff. 143 and 146\endash 148. The remaining leaves have a collection of recipes in German and Latin for preparing sealing, caulking, and adhesive compounds, suitable for stopping or repairing objects made of glass, clay, and stone; another group contains smelting and refining procedures, with this note on f. 145v: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Anno 1650 wart in Brabant ein minie gefunden, die\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 wart also geprobiert\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 A few recipes for making analytical substances, here called \ldblquote Cement,\rdblquote  and for lead glass follow, with modern pencil notes on f. 150r listing names which occur in the manuscript, the verso pasted down.}

