{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Paper codex in Latin and German, partly in cipher, 4\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 o\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , 210 x 155, ff. 132 with modern pencil foliation, consisting of three separate elements: the first (ff. 1\endash 29) of four quires written in 1536 in red and black in a {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5704[1]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 gothic cursive}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (1)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\endash 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (2\endash 3)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (4)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ; the second (ff. 30\endash 65) of three quires written in a well-controlled gothic cursive without color, (5\endash 7)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ; and the third (ff. 66\endash 132) of seven quires written in one or possibly two scrawling gothic cursives, with red headings on ff. 109\endash 124, (8)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 16\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (9\endash 10)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (11)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10+1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (12\endash 14)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . The pastedown and its conjugate flyleaf at beginning with notes by {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 Martin R\u246 \'f6sel}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  and three blank flyleaves and pastedown with further notes by R\u246 \'f6sel at end do not form parts of the collation. Written space averages 180 x 140, in single columns, occasional double- and triple-column notes, all 21\endash 35 lines per page. At least five scripts described above (but see Summary), plus two different, very attractive humanistic hands by R\u246 \'f6sel, who also appears to be the scribe responsible for late sixteenth-century additions in cipher. Marginal drawings of alchemical apparatus are cropped, as also marginalia, usually in the later italics, and corrections by several hands. Inks range from light brown to near black, with brown and red rubrics, red captions. Several initials illuminated in trick have been cut from a late fifteenth-century MS and pasted into the present MS at ff. 2v, 4v, 5, 10r, and 16r. Two overpatch addenda, on 31v and 32r, and much correction, underscoring, and interpolation of textual omission, are mostly by R\u246 \'f6sel. Moderate standard abbreviation. Three distinct papers: the first for quires (1\endash 4) with unidentified eagle watermark somewhat resembling Briquet 104 of c. 1537; the second beginning at f. 30r of a crown pattern resembling Briquet 4921 and 1922 of c. 1500; the third of the Paschal lamb resembling Briquet 61 of c. 1584, beginning at f. 66.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Early ownership of the sections of the codex dating from the first half of the sixteenth century is unknown, but one of them was written in 1536 by Wolfgang, organist at Usedom in Pomerania; the earlier elements of the volume came into the possession of Martin R\u246 \'f6sel von Rosenthal, 1586, with his ownership entry at f. 2r; shelf-number \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 A 29\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  from an early collection written on the backstrip of the binding; {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[3].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 Denis Duveen}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , with his inked number 9 on front pastedown, acquired from {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[4].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 H. P. Kraus, Inc. (bookseller), New York}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , 1949; Mellon MS 8, acquired with the Duveen collection. De Ricci-Bond 2 (8).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 SUMMARY: The contents of MS 27 combine a large number of practical procedures, chiefly alchemical but sometimes medical, with a few standard medieval alchemical texts, a juxtaposition already observed in early manuscripts in the Mellon collection, e.g., MSS 2, 5, 6, etc. Occasionally there are passages in cipher, added by Martin R\u246 \'f6sel long after the principal contents were written; the cipher seems to be of a simple number-substitution type. It is not certain how many different hands have contributed to the writing of the codex. ff. 66\endash 108 and ff. 109\endash 132 are written in the same kind of gothic cursive and are usually very careless and inelegant, so that all of these leaves may be the work of a single copyist, and it is not impossible that this same hand is that of {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[1].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 Wolfgang of Usedom}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  who wrote the first component of the codex, and whose writing of Latin is also careless and inelegant to the point of illiteracy. The writing of ff. 30\endash 65 is, however, the work of another copyist, who wrote a skillful cursive. Although all of the papers in the manuscript are of South German, Augsburg types, the colophon written by Wolfgang the organist suggests (but does not positively state) that he wrote it in his native Usedom, on the island in the Oder estuary across from Stettin; and if Rosenthal, where Martin R\u246 \'f6sel had the volume in 1586, is the town near Marburg, then MS 27 may have wandered considerable distances in the sixteenth century. A very curious feature of the handiwork of Wolfgang the organist may be noted: he cut out decorated initials partly illuminated in trick from some other manuscript source and adorned his own manuscript with them; despite his near illiteracy and the clumsiness of his copy, he did not hesitate to claim authorship of a well known text, for which R\u246 \'f6sel soundly berated him on f. 22v.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Bound, probably {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5060[0].5064/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 c. 1586}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  for {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22\chcbpat17 Martin R\u246 \'f6sel}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , in red-stained limp parchment (most of the stain now lost), single central clasp and catch now missing from center of fore-edges, two slits on each fore-edge for thong or ribbon ties, also missing.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Front pastedown: This and the facing conjugate flyleaf have notes in Martin R\u246 \'f6sel\rquote s hand, chiefly in Latin, but with some words in German, Latin verses on the flyleaf, recto, and a brief\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  De lapide philosophorum \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 on the verso. On f. 1r, which is otherwise blank, is a recipe in R\u246 \'f6sel\rquote s hand, followed by a modern shelf-mark in pencil:\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  I.N.28\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; other pencil notes, perhaps all by booksellers, on the front pastedown.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 2r) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 27.1: {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 Alexander Magnus}}\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 , {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezwrk[0].6930/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 Luna plena}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in Latin. In the originally blank margin at foot is the ownership inscription written in a fine italic hand:\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Sum ex libris Martini R\u246 \'f6selij a Rosenthal. Anno 1586. \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 The text begins, line 1: >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Incipit Alexander Magnus eximius experimentator artis divine ostendens viam veritatis. Memoratus primum \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [sic]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  in hoc libro me de luna, que omnibus donator humoribus \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [corrected from:\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  humeribus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  et est aqua et due \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [sic]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  aquae \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [corrected from:\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  aque\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  extracte sicut\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < \u8230 \'85 (f. 4r, 22) \u8230 \'85\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  et sapientibus et prudentibus. Et ea parvulis revelavit etcetera.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezlit[0].8330/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 TK}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  866 cites only Munich MS 26059, dated 1507, where the text is anonymous, and the opening varies somewhat from the present version, which may be garbled.}

