\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 19[1]r) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 30.1: Anonymous, Fifteen alchemical procedures\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in German. Heading: >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Von zinober zuo machen. Item.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 <\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Wilt du zinober machen, so n\u255 \'ffm ain tail des schwebels \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 \u8230 \'85 This passage opens a series of alchemical procedures, ending f. 26(8)v: \u8230 \'85\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  So wirt es ganntz wey\u223 \'df. \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Remainder of the page blank. \endash  Fifteen alchemical procedures, in German, for making cinnabar (four), artificial malachite or \ldblquote Spongrien\rdblquote  (five), white lead (two), for whitening silver (two), and for whitening copper (two). All unidentified.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 27 [9]r) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 30.2: {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 Pseudo-Peter Kertzenmacher}}\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 , {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezwrk[0].6930/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 Alchimie und Bergwerk}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , here titled\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Das buoch der alchame\u255 \'ff.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  Heading: >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Hie hept an das buoch der alchame\u255 \'ff.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 < Text: >\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 In dem namen Gottes. Amen.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 <\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Alle kunst kompt von Gott vnd is by im on ennde. Er t\u252 \'fct sie mittaylen allen, den diese begeren. Aristotiles spricht: Ain yeglich mensch ist von nature des genaiget\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 Ends f. 87(69)v, 13: \u8230 \'85\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  vnd nit mer schoumpt, so fl\u252 \'fc\u223 \'dft er denn on rouch als ain wachsz.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \endash  The text was printed anonymously at Strasbourg by Cammerlander in 1539; when reprinted in 1570 an added preface was signed by Peter Kertzenmacher, as noted by Ferguson 1, 19. In this copy the text proceeds through Capitel Cviij, whereas the printed versions end at Cj, and other differences suggest that this copy is entirely independent of the printed editions.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 (f. 88[70]r) \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 30.3: Anonymous, \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 List of alchemical substances\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , in German and Latin. This page opens a list of names for substances used in alchemy, written in two columns; on the left a list of words is written, and these are bracketed to a single German equivalent written in red in the right column. Ends f. 93(75)v, not transcribed. \endash  The left column supplies Latin, arabic-derived, what seem to be \ldblquote secret,\rdblquote  and occasionally German expressions, which are explained by a single clear name in German for the substance in the right column. Unidentified.}

