\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Paper   \u183 \'b7   ff. {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5706[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 152}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26    \u183 \'b7   {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5360[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 197 x 155}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26    \u183 \'b7   Germany   \u183 \'b7   1562}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3240\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Paper codex in German and Latin, 4\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 o\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , 197 x 155, ff. 152, numbered \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 1\endash 154\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  by an early hand (omitting ff. 98 and 102), the leaves numbered \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 13\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 17\endash 20\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 82\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , and \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 140\endash 145\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  now missing, the first end flyleaf numbered \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 155\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ; the original foliation, which is partly defective through water-staining and wear in the early leaves, employed in the description following. No signatures, occasional catchwords at quire-endings. Collation: (1)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (2)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 12\endash 5\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (3\endash 10)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 4\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (11\endash 13)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (14)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\endash 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (15\endash 20)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (21)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\endash 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (22)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . Calligraphically written throughout in single columns 136\endash 165 x 123\endash 136 averaging 17\endash 22 lines by a highly skilled hand using a variety of \ldblquote Fraktur\rdblquote , secretary cursive, and italic scripts in brown inks, three-column table on last leaf. No color, minimal abbreviation, but progressively frequent use of alchemical symbols; full-page drawing of a double coat of arms with inscriptions on first front flyleaf recto described below, and a small drawing at foot of f. 53v, both probably by the same skilled hand, possibly the copyist of the codex. At least two papers with unidentified heraldic watermarks in folds. Somewhat damaged, especially at beginning, by water-staining and fraying, with some leaves wanting, but the remainder entirely legible and without substantial losses.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written for the original owners whose unidentified arms, described below, are finely drawn on the front flyleaf, recto, with the initials \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 I.W.G.W.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  and \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 I.W.D.G.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , with the date \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 1562\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ; Denis Duveen, with his inked number 89, acquired from Dr. Ernst Weil (bookseller), London; Mellon MS 110, acquired with the Duveen collection. De Ricci-Bond 23 (110).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 SUMMARY: The contents of Mellon MS 40 may be compared with other manuscripts of the \ldblquote Kunstb\u252 \'fcchlein\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Probierb\u252 \'fcchlein\rdblquote  type in the Mellon collection, such as Mellon MSS 20 (an early, non-German example), 30, 38, 44, and 92. This example, while a little defective, is distinguished by the very fine armorial drawing at the beginning and the unusually handsome calligraphy, unquestionably the work of a professional master. The armorial drawing has been cited by E. T. Parker, Old Master Drawings, VI, 1931, pp. 55\endash 56, as a work of the unidentified monogrammist \ldblquote A. S.,\rdblquote  and it has been suggested that the whole manuscript is a product of the same hand. The amusing drawing accompanying the coat of arms, a woman apparently scratching herself with the legend \ldblquote The flea bites me,\rdblquote  lends a special flavor and charm to this codex.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Original binding of limp parchment stained olive green, now worn and with most of the spine, originally with three bands, missing; plain edges. Preserved in a modern box of boards with linen back, modern leather title label added.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Front pastedown: Blank, except for Duveen\rquote s characteristic number and a pencil scribble: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Manuscript\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . First front flyleaf recto: The entire page is devoted to an elaborate pen-drawing of a double coat of arms, probably of a husband and wife of minor German noble families. Above the left coat are the letters \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 I.W.G.W\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . and above the right, \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 I.W.D.G.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , while the date \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 1562\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . is written below and between the letters. Standing in a slight landscape with ruined buildings below and between the two coats of arms is a female figure seen in left profile wearing a long dress; in her lowered right hand she holds a banner which bears an inscription: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Mich beisset der floch\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  i.e., \ldblquote The flea bites me\rdblquote ]. Her left hand has raised the skirt of her dress and is concealed beneath it. The drawing, which is of excellent quality, is unsigned. On the verso of this leaf and the recto of the second front flyleaf is a long entry by a later German hand, perhaps reading notes, as \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Theophrastus libro archidoxis. Folio J093\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 is cited. The second front flyleaf, verso, and f. 1 are entirely blank.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 f. 2r, 1 \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 An untitled Kunstb\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 \u252 \'fc\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 chlein\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Wenn du wildt Rauch amlieren, es sej von vogeln oder Tieren, oder ob du rauch gewandt an bilden woltstest machen\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 The text deals first with several procedures involving treatments of glass, including gilding and silvering glass, then turns to metallurgy, describing numerous procedures for separating and purifying metals, then the coloring or tinting of metal surfaces including mention of French and English methods. The preparation of various metals for different processes is discussed, followed by further directions for gilding and silvering. On f. 41r \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Je\u246 \'f6rg Preus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  is mentioned, and a recipe obtained through the generosity of Brother \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Augustinus vonn Brisenshause\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  is spoken of on f. 71v. A recipe by \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Meister Heinnrich Petersillig zu Halberstadt\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  is written on f. 73v, and a longer procedure begins on f. 125v, 10: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Die ware Kunst grundtlichen zusamen gezogen, meinen kindernn antzuzeigen\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 A Zement Hieoronimi \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 [sic]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Doctoris\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  is cited on f. 149r. The text ends with a recipe for the coagulation of Mercury on f. 154v, followed by additional notes in a later hand and, on the first end flyleaf recto (numbered f. 155) is a table of alchemical symbols possibly by the original copyist. The second end flyleaf, which is blank, is pasted to the first, there is no end pastedown, and the inside surface of the lower cover is blank. \endash  An untitled Kunstb\u252 \'fcchlein containing hundreds of recipes for a great variety of alchemical processes, chiefly metallurgical, dated 1562 on the front flyleaf which contains an elaborate drawing of the arms of the original owners.}

