\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Paper codex in German, 4\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 o\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , 175 x 145, ff. 148, of which f. 1 is ruled but otherwise blank and pasted down, f. 2 is ruled but otherwise blank, f. 3 is unnumbered and contains a title on the recto (verso blank), and f. 4 is blank but ruled; ff. 5\endash 123 are correctly numbered \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 1\endash 119\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  by the copyist, and the remaining ff. 124\endash 148 are unnumbered. In the following description the correct folio number is cited first, with the copyist\rquote s foliation where applicable following the correct foliation and in parentheses. No signatures, catchwords at every page. Collation: (1)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8+1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (2)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (3\endash 10)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\endash 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (11\endash 12)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (13\endash 16)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\endash 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (17)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , (18)\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 6+1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . Written throughout in single columns, 105 x 98, averaging 16 lines without headlines, right and left bordering lines often in red ink, but without ruling, by a single copyist writing a practiced German secretary hand, partly in a modified \ldblquote Fraktur\rdblquote , partly in a more italic cursive, and occasionally (as in the title page) in a formal gothic script; a few additions at the end in a later hand; brown inks, red inks used for tabular data and infrequent sketches of alchemical apparatus in the text; no corrections noted, standard abbreviation. Paper with an unidentified, complex circular watermark in the folds; lower margins slightly wormed at beginning, not affecting text.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Original ownership unknown; penciled marks and price inside front cover in an American bookseller\rquote s hand; Denis Duveen, with his inked number 79, acquired from Dr. Ernst Weil (bookseller), London, 1949; Mellon MS 104, acquired with the Duveen collection. De Ricci-Bond 22 (104).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 
\par \pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 SUMMARY: Although the 1532 date has long been accepted as the date of this copy, it seems certain that Mellon MS 44 is a transcript executed toward the end of the sixteenth century of an unidentified original. On f. 42(38)v is a process ascribed to a certain Bernhard Schaller of N\u252 \'fcrnberg, dated 15 November 1544; \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Thomas Gregorius\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  is mentioned on f. 121(117)v, \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Aldendorff in Hessen\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  on f. 139(135)v, and Hans Knabe, \ldblquote who lived in a little town called L\u228 \'e4merstatt not far from Troppau,\rdblquote  [K. S.: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Dieser Hans Knabe gewohnt an der Schlesischen grentze im M\u228 \'e4hrischen geb\u252 \'fcrge in einem St\u228 \'e4ttlein mit Nahmen L\u228 \'e4merstatt nicht weit von Troppau.\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 ] on f. 144(140)v. All of these individuals are unidentified. A metallurgical experiment book of the type circulated among those interested in alchemy, mining, and metallurgy in the sixteenth century, this text treats at length several minerals with a metallic luster such as marcasite, especially crystallized iron pyrites, which were often used ornamentally, in costume jewelry, at the time of composition of this codex, when they were considered semiprecious materials. Andreas Oberlender has not been identified, and the text may be unpublished. Mellon MS 44 is to be compared with the 1557 \ldblquote Vade mecum\rdblquote  by Caspar Hartung vom Hoff, Mellon MS 38 of the Mellon collection, and the \ldblquote Kunstb\u252 \'fcchlein\rdblquote  by A.S., dated 1562, Mellon MS 40. Mellon MS 92 is also similar, both because it has a large number of metallurgical recipes, and because it has long been considered a compilation by a certain Wachsmuth, dated 1553, while in reality it is a much later copy.}

