\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Paper (lightweight and brittle; watermarks buried in gutter and obscured by text), ff. 42 + ii (paper), 190 x 160 (ca. 145 x 120) mm. 15\endash 27 long lines. Not ruled   \u183 \'b7   I\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  (\endash 1), II\endash III\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 6\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , IV\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 10\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  (\endash 9 ?), V\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 4\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 , VI\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 8\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  (\endash 1 ?), VII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 2\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22    \u183 \'b7   Written in gothic textura and cursive by many scribes. Scribe 1 (ff. 1r\endash 17v) writes in a large gothic textura. Scribe 2 (ff. 18r\endash 27r) writes in an even cursive script. Other cursive hands make notes and additions on ff. 1r\endash 27r, and are intermingled in the text of ff. 27v\endash 42v   \u183 \'b7   Elaborate capitals on ff. 1r\endash 17v, all by the first scribe, in brown ink. On ff. 18r\endash 26v, headings in gothic textura, square capitals, or humanistic script, above the cursive text. Musical notation on a 5-line staff on ff. 15r\endash 16v (the Lord's Prayer)   \u183 \'b7   Most of the folios are now detached from the binding; some attempt was made to repair this, using paper strips. Margin of f. 1 repaired; does not affect the text.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written in Germany in the 17th century, judging from the script; many later additions and corrections. Early provenance unknown. Note inside front cover: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Aus dem Nachlass des Abts D. Thiele f\u252 \'fcr AGrube\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  [sic]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 , Braunschweig d 9. Oct. 1886\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 .; probably Heinrich August Ludwig Thiele (1814\endash 1886), Lutheran theologian and Abbot of the monastery of Riddagshausen (Allgmeine Deutsche Biographie, v. 38, pp. 750\endash 753). Presented to Yale in 1948 by Maybelle M. von Kalinowski.}

