{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red153\green0\blue204;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Parchment   \u183 \'b7   1 f.   \u183 \'b7   326 x 262 (265 x 212) mm   \u183 \'b7   2 columns of 29 lines. Rulings in hard point on the flesh side barely visible   \u183 \'b7   Written in elegant Luxeuil minuscule, with headings in uncials   \u183 \'b7   Large initial at beginning of each verse filled with yellow, red, and/or green; headings in green or red   \u183 \'b7   neumes   \u183 \'b7   Removed from a bookbinding; text suffers from holes, stains, creases, and repairs.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written at Luxeuil or one of its affiliated houses at the beginning of the 8th century. Most of the remaining fragments (except for those in Munich which are from the binding of CLM 17738 from Stadtamhof near Regensburg [see Lowe, Codices latini antiquiores, vol. 9, see below, p. 29, no. 1337]) may be traced to the Benedictine abbey of Admont in Austria. The handwritten inventory of Jacob Wichner (Catalogus codicum manu scriptorium Admontensis, Admont 1888, p. 330, no. 12) contains a marginal notation that three leaves \ldblquote Fragmenta Ezechielis et Jeremiae, Perg. VIII. Jhrh.\rdblquote  were acquired by Goldschmidt\rquote s of London in 1936. The Yale fragment was presumably one of these since it was bought from Goldschmidt\rquote s in 1938 by Clarence Mendell who presented it to the Branford College Library of Yale University. Deposited in the University Library in 1953.
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 Fifteen folios from the original codex survive; for bibliography on the Beinecke fragment and for information concerning its relationship to the other leaves see Lowe, Codices latini antiquiores, vol. 11, see below, p. 20, no. **1337 (eleven fragments listed; four leaves appeared in the sale catalogue of Sotheby Parke Bernet and Company, Catalogue of Twenty Western Illuminated Manuscripts from the Fifth to the Fifteenth century, 1982, pp. 16\endash 19).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 Most of the verses have received neumes. According to K. D. Hartzell these were certainly added much later than the leaf\rquote s own date. The ink is a medium brown which has been rubbed here and there. The neumes on the verso are much fainter than those on the recto. They were written in an unknown center by a scribe who practiced a mixed style, possibly in the eleventh century. No examples of climacus or porrectus are present. The virga\rquote s head turns to the right in the Lorraine manner, but the punctum is not the Lorraine form. The clivis is closer to the French style than the German, but the torculus is decidedly Germanic. The podatus, made in one stroke, resembles that in a Gradual-Lectionary from St. Bertin (St. Omer, Biblioth\u232 \'e8que Municipale MS 252; Pal\u233 \'e9ographie musicale, III, pl. 184b). The result is thus eclectic. Although he probably learned to write neumes from a German, the scribe modified his script under French influences.}

