{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written in Northern France at the turn of the 14th century, as indicated by the style of the decoration. The fleurs-de-lis in the backgrounds of the miniatures on ff. 25r and 55r need not refer to a member of the French royal house. Unidentified 19th-century coat of arms on f. 1r: gules, three hares' heads proper, with the motto \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Tunc satiabor\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . Collection of William Alexander Douglas, Duke of Hamilton and Brandon. Given by him in 1856 to the Reverend Walter Sneyd, according to a note formerly on a flyleaf no longer present in the manuscript (see M. R. James, op. cit., p. 1, and Hidden Friends. The Comites Latentes Collection of Illuminated Manuscripts, exh. cat., Sotheby's, 20\endash 28 September 1985, no page numbers, same page as entries 21\endash 23). Sneyd sale, Sotheby's, 16 Dec. 1903, no. 513. Bernard Quaritch, London. Collection of Edmond de Rothschild, MS 98 (his sale Paris, Palais Galliera, 24 June 1968, no. 1). Acquired from H. P. Kraus in 1968 as the gift of Edwin J. Beinecke.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Exhibition Catalogue: W. Cahn and J. Marrow, eds., Medieval and Renaissance Manuscripts at Yale. A Selection, in: Yale University Library Gazette 52 (1978), pp. 202\endash 203, no. 29, pl. 12 of f. 84r   \u183 \'b7   A. N. L. Munby, Connoisseurs and Medieval Miniatures, Oxford 1972, p. 114   \u183 \'b7   R. W. Pfaff, Montague Rhodes James, London, 1980, pp. 195\endash 196   \u183 \'b7   Jeffrey F. Hamburger, The Rothschild Canticles. Art and Mysticism in Flanders and the Rhineland circa 1300, New Haven: Yale University Press 1990   \u183 \'b7   Barbara A. Shailor, The Medieval Book. Illustrated from the Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library (catalogue of an exhibition held Aug. 15 \endash  Oct. 31, 1988), Toronto 1991, p. 81 (ff. 65v\endash 66r) and 85.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Binding: ca. 1966. Bound in two volumes (I: ff. 1\endash 96; II: ff. 97\endash 192) in native tanned vermilion Nigerian goatskin, by J. Greenfield, without any adhesive touching the bookblock itself. Previously bound in brown leather in a single volume.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 f. 1r\endash 107r \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Florilegium comprised of a series of meditations and prayers\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . f. 1r Unidentifed arms, later addition; ff. 1v\endash 4r, 5r, 6v\endash 7r, 8r, 9r, 10r, 11r have tinted drawings added s. XIV\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; (ff. 12v\endash 105v) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Emitte agnum domine dominatorem terre de petra deserti ad montem filie syon\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Bernardus orauit domine duc me ubi es. dixit ei barnarde non facio quoniam si ducerem te ubi sum annichilareris michi et tibi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . ff. 4v, 5v\endash 6r, 7v, 8v, 9v, 10v, 11v\endash 12r, 13v, 15v\endash 16r, 17r, 18r, 19v\endash 20r, 21v\endash 22r, 23v\endash 24r, 25v\endash 26r, 27v, 28v\endash 29r, 30v, 31v, 32v\endash 33r, 34v\endash 35r, 36v\endash 37r, 38v\endash 39r, 40v\endash 41r, 42v\endash 43r, 44v\endash 45r, 46v\endash 47r, 48v\endash 49r, 50v, 51v, 52v, 53v\endash 54r, 55v\endash 56r, 57v\endash 58r, 59v\endash 60r, 61v\endash 62r, 63r, 64v\endash 65r, 66v\endash 67r, 68v\endash 69r, 70v\endash 71r, 72r, 73v\endash 74r, 75v\endash 76r, 77v\endash 78r, 79v\endash 80r, 81v\endash 82r, 83v, 84v\endash 85r, 86r, 87r, 88v\endash 89r, 90v\endash 91r, 92v\endash 93r, 94v\endash 95r, 96v\endash 97r, 98v\endash 99r, 100v\endash 101r, 102v\endash 103r, 104v\endash 105r, 106v blank; f. 107r and upper portion of 107v are erased (see ff. 133v\endash 140v). \endash  The text, apparently a unicum, is a cento of biblical, liturgical, and patristic citations, with some additional material spuriously attributed to St. Bernard. The most important sources are the Song of Songs, the other Wisdom books, the Prophets, and, in the Trinitarian section, Augustine's De Trinitate. With the exception of f. 14r, the text occurs only on alternating versos. For a more complete description of the text and layout, see M. R. James, Description of an Illuminated Manuscript of the XIIIth Century in the Possession of Bernard Quaritch, London 1904, which is, in part, inaccurate, and Jeffrey F. Hamburger, The Rothschild Canticles. Art and Mysticism in Flanders and the Rhineland circa 1300, New Haven: Yale University Press 1990, which includes an edition of this text.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 107v\endash 112v \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Unidentified tract on the vices and virtues\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Sub superbia continentur hec qui secuntur. Inanis gloria que est appetitus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  (f. 108r) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 laudis humane\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Caritas est uirtus quam homo diligit deum super se et super omnia propter deum et proximum suum sicut se ipsum propter deum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 .}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 113r\endash 114v \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Tract on the monstrous races\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 In damasco erant diuerse herbe de natura speciali\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 adam erat pauperrimus hominum quia mandatum dei trangressus est et promeruit mortem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \endash  R. A. Wisby, Marvels of the East in the Weiner Genesis and in Wolfram's Parzifal, in: Essays in German and Dutch Literature, ed. W. Robson-Scott, London 1973, pp. 9\endash 10 and n. 40; J. B. Friedman, The Monstrous Races in Medieval Art and Thought, Cambridge, Mass. 1981, p. 94, 214, n. 25, and 233, n. 12; and H. W. Janson, Apes and Ape-Lore in the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, New York 1957, p. 94.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 115r\endash 119v \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Seven exempla\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Nota quod quidem nobilis mulier cum esset in ecclesia tempore hyemali quedam paupercula mulier post tergum suum gemebat pre angustia frigoris\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... (f. 119r) Final exemplum: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Nota de clerico dormienti cui uidebatur quod demones ducerent animam eius ad infernum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 vidit ibi omnia peccata sua que confessus est intrauit religionem\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 .}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 120r\endash 121r \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Excerpts attributed to John Chrysostom, Augustine, and Bernard, from an unidentified florilegium\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Crisostomus otium mors est et uiui hominis sepultura\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Augustinus in epistola ad yponenses. si modo tanta custodia tanta intentione cum magno labore agitis ne in aliquos cruciatus transitorios incidatis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . ff. 122r, 123r tinted drawings; ff. 121v, 122v, 123v blank.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 124r\endash 132r \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Pharetra\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , excerpts. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Acceptio personarum iudicare digne de subditis nequeunt qui in subditorum causis sua uel odia uel gratiam secuntur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 postremus gradus est omnium uitiorum peremptorius ut de hoc mundo credas te cotidie migraturum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . f. 132v blank. \endash  Excerpts from the Pharetra, a florilegium probably composed by an anonymous Franciscan working before 1264 (see R. H. and M. Rouse, Preachers, Florilegia and Sermons. Studies in the "Manipulus florum" of Thomas of Ireland, Toronto 1979, pp. 41 and 204\endash 205). For a printed edition of the text, see Bonaventure, Opera omnia, Vatican City 1596, v. 6 (Part 8), pp. 103\endash 208.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 f. 133r \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Moral sayings, including verses on the confession of sins\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Omnis mulier fornicaria est quasi stercus in uia comes \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ...\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26  Illa autem aggrauant peccatum et hoc scitur per hos uersus. Aggrauat ordo locus peccata scientia tempus. Etas conditio numerus mora copia causa et modus in culpa status altus lucta pusilla.}

