\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Parchment   \u183 \'b7   {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5706[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 1}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  f.   \u183 \'b7   {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5360[0]/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26\chcbpat17 118 x 184}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26    \u183 \'b7   Italy ? / [K. S.: Holy Land ?]   \u183 \'b7   s. XII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1/4\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [K. S.: XII\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 1\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Parchment   \u183 \'b7   1 folio   \u183 \'b7   118 x 184 mm   \u183 \'b7   1 column   \u183 \'b7   20 lines. Dry-point ruling; two full sets of text rulings, one horizontal (for this text) and the other vertical, suggesting that the parchment was originally intended for another purpose   \u183 \'b7   The Guide is written in late Caroline minuscule. The charm is written in a thirteenth-century [K. S.: at the end of the 12th century] gothic hand (littera textualis), evidently in Germany [K. S.: Southeastern Germany]   \u183 \'b7   At the beginning of the text is a cross with ornamentation in brown penwork. 2-line initial A in brown ink with the left shaft and cross bar hollow and the right shaft solid. 1-line initials are in brown rustic capitals. Punctuation consists of the punctus and punctus versus.}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg0\uc1\deff0\deflang0\deflangfe0{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset1 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue128;\red0\green128\blue128;\red0\green128\blue0;\red128\green0\blue128;\red128\green0\blue0;\red128\green128\blue0;\red128\green128\blue128;\red192\green192\blue192;\red204\green238\blue221;}

\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 According to the description prepared by H. P. Kraus, Inc., Bernhard Bischoff considered the script \ldblquote a sort of amulet or souvenir written in the Holy Land, in the early decades of the Jerusalem Kingdom.\rdblquote  We have no record of the document in which Bischoff made this attribution. The script resembles early twelfth-century Italian hands. [K. S.: Bischoff also states that the text was evidently written by a person of little education, since the text abounds in grammatical errors, who was a crusader or a pilgrim (see Davis, A Twelfth-Century Pilgrim\rquote s Guide, p. 15).]
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 The text gives a reference to the capture of Jerusalem by the Frankish army on 15 July 1099. There is also a mention of the resting place outside of Jerusalem of Abraham, Isaac and others, suggesting the text was written after 1119 when the tombs of the patriarchs were discovered in Hebron (see Davis, A Twelfth-Century Pilgrim\rquote s Guide, p. 18). The text of the Guide shows some similarities with the Ottobonian Guide (Vatican, Ottobon. lat. 169) which was probably written between 1103\endash 1113 (see Davis, A Twelfth-Century Pilgrim\rquote s Guide, pp. 15\endash 18). [K. S.: Shagrir assumes that the text is probably a copy made close to the time of the original writing (see Shagrir, Guide, p. 2).]
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 A modern hand has written the number \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 96\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  in the lower margin of the recto.
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 Zinniker 264.}

