\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 Written in Germany at the beginning of the 14th century according to K. Schneider; it was part of a manuscript containing not only Wolfram's Willehalm but also the Willehalm of Ulrich von dem T\u252 \'fcrlin. In addition to MS 486, two fragments survive: Munich, Universit\u228 \'e4tsbibliothek 4\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24 o\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  Cod. ms. 889 and Strasbourg, Bibl. Nationale et Universitaire MS 2201 (cf. Schr\u246 \'f6der, op. cit., pp. lvii\endash lviii, no. 52). According to H. Fromm (Ein wiedergefundenes Willehalm-Fragment, in: Zeitschrift f\u252 \'fcr deutsches Altertum und deutsche Literatur 103,2 [1974], pp. 123\endash 125) the original manuscript was dismembered around 1550 and the Beinecke fragment used as a binding for Sphaera mvndi, autore Rabbi Abrahamo Hispano filio R. Haijae. Arithmetica secundum omnes species suas autore Rabbi Elija Orientali. Quos libros Oswaldus Schreckenfuchsius vertit in linguam latinam, Sebastianus vero Munsterus illustravit annotationibus, Basel 1546. Inscription on Beinecke fragment on what was the spine reads: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Sphaera mundi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 / \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 Arithmeti\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 / \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 ca Rabi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22 . Inscription, s. xviii, \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs22 pro. 3 xr aus der Meehlf\u252 \'fchrer. B\u252 \'fccher Auction\plain \f1\lang1033\fs22  on f. 1r, upper margin; this probably refers to Rudolf Martin Meelf\u252 \'fchrer (1670\endash 1729; Biographie Universelle, Paris 1821, v. 28, pp. 103\endash 104). Belonged to Edward Dowden (1843\endash 1913; DNB, 1912\endash 1921, p. 126); autograph letter (27 July 1891) in library files from Richard Garnett to Prof. Dowden stating that Mr. Ward of the Manuscript Department of the British Museum had identified the leaves and citing the text in the Lachmann edition, Berlin 1833, pp. 530\endash 538. P. M. Barnard Sale, Tunbridge Wells (Cat. 122, 1920, no. 166). Belonged to Wilfred Merton (1889\endash 1957), who according to Fromm sold the fragment during his lifetime since it is not listed in the catalogue of his estate. Acquired from H. P. Kraus in 1965 (Cat. 88, no. 12; Cat. 100, no. 10) as the gift of Edwin J. Beinecke.}

