\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 144r\endash 157r \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Chronicle of the World\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  in the framework of the Six Ages. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Sex diebus rerum omnium creaturas Deus formavit. Prima die condidit celum et terram et firmamentum celi\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 \u167 \'a7 Bonifacius papa IX prius vocatus Pirinus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . \endash  Opens with Isidore of Seville (Isidorus Hispalensis\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ), \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 Chronica\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ,\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  T. Mommsen, ed., MGH, AA, v. 11, 2, 1894, pp. 424 ff. The Sixth Age begins f. 147vb and initially alternates series of Emperors (\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Imperatores\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ) with series of Popes (\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Pontifices\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ). This section ends f. 152r with the accession of Charlemagne and with Pope Hormisdas (514\endash 523). The subsequent Emperors and rulers of Italy are simply listed f. 152rb, but the compiler has noted: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quere prologum in sequentes et precedentes imperatores in inferiori spacio presentis folii ipsum vertendo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 item verte sex folia et invenies nomina imperatorum Francorum et Italorum et Theotunicorum qui prefuerunt Romano imperio\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ; this refers to the additions at the bottom of the pages and to ff. 157v\endash 158r. The final section is a history of the Popes from John I (523\endash 526) to Boniface IX (1389\endash 1404). As the latter is only mentioned, while his predecessor Urban VI (1370\endash 1389) is treated at length, the text must have been compiled under his pontificate. There are ample additions in the lower margins of most pages.}

