\pard\fi0\li0\tx705\tx1410\tx2130\tx2835\tx3540\tx4245\tx4950\tx5670\tx6375\tx7080\tx7785\tx8490\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ff. 158v\endash 161v \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 Thomas de Cantiprato, De natura reru\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 m\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , Lib. 17 and 18\plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs26 .\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De sideribus et planetis. Post de septem regionibus. Deinde de passionibus aeris, de ventis, de nubibus et nebula et iride et elementis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [= ff. 158v\endash 161v and 170r\endash 172v]. \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Generaliter primo dicendum est de spera et motu eius\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Spere motus duobus axibus volvitur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Generaliter primo dicendum est de septem planetis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... (f. 159r) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quedam notabilia generalia huic tamen collecta\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... [on the planets]. (f. 160v) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De passionibus aeris et primo de tonitruo. Tonitruum secundum philosophos est quando venti suo spiramine\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... (f. 161r) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De casu stellarum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . [\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 S\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 telle aliquando cadere videntur in aere\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quid sit ventus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . [\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 G\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 eneraliter primo videndum est quid sit ventus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... (f. 161v) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De nubibus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . [\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 N\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ubes dicuntur quasi nimborum naves\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De nebula\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . [\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 N\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ebula fit dum humidum\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  [sic] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 exalaciones vaporabiliter trahuntur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 De iride arcu celeste\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 . [\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 I\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 ]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 ris sive arcus celestis creatur secundum Aristotelem ex radio solis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26  ... \endash  Astronomical and meteorological notes extracted from Thomas of Cantimpr\u233 \'e9, De natura rerum, Books 17 and 18. At the end, in the compiler\rquote s hand: \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs26 Quere alia verba de iride huc pertinentia vertendo octo folia versus dexteram et invenies ea\plain \f1\lang1033\fs26 , referring to ff. 170r\endash 172v.}

